{"name":"Brandenburg Gate","hex":"#706f64","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"Brandenburg Gate","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Proper noun","definitions":["An 18th-century neoclassical monument in Berlin, built after the restoration of order during the early Batavian Revolution, and now a major landmark."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/Brandenburg_Gate"}],"entries":[{"title":"Brandenburg Gate","blocks":[{"text":"The Brandenburg Gate is an 18th-century neoclassical monument in Berlin, Germany. One of the best-known landmarks of the country, it was erected on the site of a former city gate that marked the start of the road from Berlin to Brandenburg an der Havel, the former capital of the Margraviate of Brandenburg. The current structure was built from 1788 to 1791 by orders of King Frederick William II of Prussia, based on designs by the royal architect Carl Gotthard Langhans. The bronze sculpture of the quadriga crowning the gate is a work by the sculptor Johann Gottfried Schadow."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a6/Brandenburger_Tor_abends.jpg/330px-Brandenburger_Tor_abends.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brandenburg_Gate"},{"title":"Brandenburg Gate (Potsdam)","blocks":[{"text":"The Potsdam Gate on the Luisenplatz in Potsdam, was built in 1770–71 by Carl von Gontard and Georg Christian Unger by order of Frederick II of Prussia, to celebrate his several victories in the Seven Years' War."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a1/Brandenburger_Tor_Potsdam_November_2013.jpg/330px-Brandenburger_Tor_Potsdam_November_2013.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brandenburg_Gate_(Potsdam)"},{"title":"Brandenburg","blocks":[{"text":"Brandenburg, officially the State of Brandenburg, is a landlocked state in northeastern Germany. Brandenburg borders Poland and the states of Berlin, Mecklenburg-Vorpommern, Lower Saxony, Saxony-Anhalt, and Saxony. It is the fifth-largest German state by area and the tenth-most populous, with 2.5 million residents. Potsdam is the state capital and largest city. Other major towns are Cottbus, Brandenburg an der Havel and Frankfurt (Oder)."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/01/Flag_of_Brandenburg.svg/330px-Flag_of_Brandenburg.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brandenburg"},{"title":"Gate","blocks":[{"text":"A gate or gateway is a point of entry to or from a space enclosed by walls or other barriers. The word is derived from Proto-Germanic *gatan, meaning an opening or passageway. Synonyms include portal. The concept originally referred to the gap or hole in the wall or fence, rather than a barrier which closed it. Gates may prevent or control the entry or exit of individuals, or they may be merely decorative. The moving part or parts of a gateway may be considered \"doors\", as they are fixed at one side whilst opening and closing like one."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/94/Gate_of_the_BCR_Headquarters_Building_%28Bucharest%2C_Romania%29.jpg/330px-Gate_of_the_BCR_Headquarters_Building_%28Bucharest%2C_Romania%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gate"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/db/1795ca_Johann-Carl-Richter_Propyleen%2BBrandenburger-Tor.jpg/500px-1795ca_Johann-Carl-Richter_Propyleen%2BBrandenburger-Tor.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/db/1795ca_Johann-Carl-Richter_Propyleen%2BBrandenburger-Tor.jpg/1280px-1795ca_Johann-Carl-Richter_Propyleen%2BBrandenburger-Tor.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/db/1795ca_Johann-Carl-Richter_Propyleen%2BBrandenburger-Tor.jpg/500px-1795ca_Johann-Carl-Richter_Propyleen%2BBrandenburger-Tor.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"An early 19th-century engraving comparing the recently constructed Brandenburg Gate (lower picture) to (an imagined restoration of) its historical model: the Propylaea of the Acropolis of Athens","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3A1795ca_Johann-Carl-Richter_Propyleen%2BBrandenburger-Tor.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ed/Berlin-Brandenburg_Gate_overview.jpg/960px-Berlin-Brandenburg_Gate_overview.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ed/Berlin-Brandenburg_Gate_overview.jpg/1280px-Berlin-Brandenburg_Gate_overview.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ed/Berlin-Brandenburg_Gate_overview.jpg/960px-Berlin-Brandenburg_Gate_overview.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ABerlin-Brandenburg_Gate_overview.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0a/00_2486_Quadriga_-_Brandenburger_Tor_%28Berlin%29.jpg/500px-00_2486_Quadriga_-_Brandenburger_Tor_%28Berlin%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0a/00_2486_Quadriga_-_Brandenburger_Tor_%28Berlin%29.jpg/960px-00_2486_Quadriga_-_Brandenburger_Tor_%28Berlin%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0a/00_2486_Quadriga_-_Brandenburger_Tor_%28Berlin%29.jpg/500px-00_2486_Quadriga_-_Brandenbu